\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Thai c.bank says has no target level for baht\b0\par\par\uc2 390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2009\par 09:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The Thai baht is trading near a 14-month high against the dollar but it remains competitive and the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to stop it rising above a certain level, an assistant governor said.\par \par \uc2 Suchada Kirakul told Reuters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to address volatility.\par \par \uc2 "We are in the market as necessary to smooth out fluctuations or to prevent a market panic," she said in an interview on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Asked about a market view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to halt the baht's rise at 33.50 per dollar, she replied: "We don't have any specific level for the baht ... or try to defend it at certain levels."\par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=TH> was around 33.59/62 to the dollar on Monday. It has risen about 3.6 percent against the dollar so far this year, making it the best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia after Indonesia's rupiah and South Korea's won.\par \par \uc2 The baht has strengthened quickly by 1.3 percent in the past three weeks against a weak dollar, and Suchada said its appreciation was also due to the country's current account surplus and capital inflows across the region.\par \par \uc2 She said the baht was still competitive and current capital flows were non-speculative.\par \par \uc2 "The baht is not at a disadvantage to trade partners and competitors," Suchada said, adding other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had also strengthened due in part to risk appetite in the region.\par \par \uc2 Suchada said the central had no need and no rush to sell more savings bonds after it raised about 131 billion baht ($3.90 billion) in savings bonds earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 However, it may do so if there was demand and if it did not affect the bond market or compete with government plans, she said. "We have to discuss with the finance ministry first."\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij told Reuters his ministry planned to sell another 50 billion baht of savings bonds in November after its bond sale in July attracted overwhelming demand from retail investors. [ID:nBKK173619]. ($1=33.58 Baht) (Reporting by Orathai Sriring; Editing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/ (URGENT, INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|ABX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091001e59s000fl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
