\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: IMF:Thai Stimulus May Curb GDP Fall,BOT To Mull Cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2009\par 12:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds trade outlook, comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves)\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The International Monetary Fund said Thailand could limit its economic contraction this year to 3% with significant fiscal stimulus, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would consider further interest-rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 But the IMF said, in an annual report Thursday on its Article IV talks with Bangkok, that risks to the near-term growth are skewed to the downside.\par \par \uc2 Among the risks are heightened political uncertainties, weak implementation of the fiscal stimulus and a potentially sharper slowdown in external demand.\par \par \uc2 "With political stability, a restoration of domestic and external confidence, and full implementation of the public investment program, Thailand would be able to return to sustainable, high growth rates over the medium term," the report said.\par \par \uc2 The IMF said a 'determined implementation' of fiscal stimulus program is critical in bolstering the economy and that the increase in deficits and public debt accumulated from implementing the program should be manageable over the medium term.\par \par \uc2 "Given the expected length and depth of the global downturn, there was agreement that fiscal policy should use the available space to remain expansionary during 2009-10," it said.\par \par \uc2 Despite ample liquidity, the government needs to make additional efforts to tap retail investor demand for longer-duration government bonds by offering higher yields or innovative products to finance its stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Both exports and imports are projected to contract sharply in inflation-adjusted terms this year. However, the fall in exports is expected to be larger, with import demand sustained by the large fiscal stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "A small current account surplus is projected for 2009 on account of an improvement in the terms of trade while the financial account is projected to turn negative," the report said.\par \par \uc2 Domestic demand is expected to be a key contributor to economic growth next year, with a substantial contribution expected from public investment. Starting in the second half of next year, a recovery in Thailand's main trading partners can be expected to stimulate a recovery in private investment and a rebound in exports.\par \par \uc2 The IMF projected that Thailand's GDP will grow 1% in 2010 and to 4% in 2011.\par \par \uc2 "Over the medium term, growth could converge to a potential rate of 5%-6%," the report said.\par \par \uc2 Baht In Line With Fundamentals\par \par \uc2 The IMF called the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's decision to sharply cut its policy rate since late 2008 as "timely".\par \par \uc2 When the IMF completed the report in April, the BOT felt that there was scope for further cuts in the policy rate, the report said.\par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also mindful that the impact of monetary policy may be limited by feedback loops from the weaker real economy and the current excess liquidity in the financial system.\par \par \uc2 "Going forward, the BOT intended to balance these considerations in determining the size and timing of further cuts in the policy rate," the report said.\par \par \uc2 The IMF also welcomed Thailand's commitment to a flexible exchange-rate system and said the level of the baht was "broadly appropriate." The report said the BOT agreed during discussions in March and April that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real effective exchange rate was in line with fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The BOT underscored its commitment to a flexible exchange rate, with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to smooth out excessive volatility, the report said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's financial and corporate sectors are in 'robust shape', but risks from the global recession and declining GDP in 2009 remain. The expected default probabilities in the corporate sector have increased since the fourth quarter of last year but remain low relative to its peers, it said.\par \par \uc2 "The likely deterioration in asset quality and balance sheets of banks could be absorbed for the foreseeable future by capital buffers. The corporate sector should also be able to ride out the downturn," the report said.\par \par \uc2 -By Phisanu Phromchanya, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com [ 27-08-09 1041GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090827e58r000nc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
