\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 - \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  on back foot as c.banks try to cap gains\b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2009\par 09:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  mostly weaker amid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  jitters\par \par \uc2 * Investors move to book profits from recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallies\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected in Taiwan, \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Singapore\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 2 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit led a broad Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreat on Tuesday as investors took profits from recent rally out of fears that more regional central banks might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep exchange rates competitive.\par \par \uc2 The dollar stabilised a day after hitting its lowest this year against the euro <EUR=> and a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  <.DXY> as fresh signs of a recovery in the global economy dented the safe-haven allure of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nT180784].\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> fell 0.6 percent to 3.492 per dollar as investors book profits from the ringgit's recent rally amid fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may follow peers to temper the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strengthening.\par \par \uc2 "We are hearing a lot of central banks are buying dollars this morning," said a trader in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 Three-month offshore dollar/ringgit non-deliverable forwards <MYRNDFOR=> edged up to 3.4865, implying a 0.1 percent ringgit fall from the spot compared to 1 percent last week.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain the rise in the Taiwan dollar <TWP=TP> while traders also suspected similar \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Singapore.\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar pulled back to 32.372 per U.S. dollar from intraday high of 32.269, a day after it hit its highest in more than seven months. [ID:nTP180462]\par \par \uc2 PESO\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> fell to 47.20 per dollar after touching a one-week high at 47 in early trade on caution over possible official dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Wariness over BSP's continued defence of the 47 level forced market players to cover their short dollar positions," said a Manila-based trader.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions to try to contain the peso's gains against the dollar, which have amounted to 3 percent since early March.\par \par \uc2 But the peso remains a laggard Asia, gaining just 0.7 percent against the dollar so far this year compared to the rise of more than 7 percent in the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=>, 3 percent in Indian rupee <INR=IN> and 2 percent in Thai baht <THB=TH>.\par \par \uc2 Three-month offshore NDFs <PHPNDFOR=> priced in a 0.6 percent peso decline from the spot compared to 0.8 percent on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The implied peso depreciation has fallen from nearly 4 percent in March. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at   0650 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        96.31     