\par\par \b \uc2 Thai PM urges banks to lend after rate cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2009\par 14:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, May 14 (Reuters) - Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has room to cut interest rates but previous reductions have failed to prod commercial banks to boost their lending, Prime Minister Abhisit Vejjajiva said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "They do, but not much," he told Reuters, when asked if the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  could trim rates again after slashing its key rate by a combined 250 basis points to 1.25 percent since December. "It's unlikely to achieve much more."\par \par \uc2 The BoT will review the rate on May 20, when analysts expect a 25 basis point cut to 1.00 percent, the lowest level since May 2000, when the bank began targeting inflation.\par \par \uc2 Abhisit said there was ample liquidity in the banking system and interest rates were low, but "clearly the banks are still reluctant to lend".\par \par \uc2 "The only thing preventing credit getting out is risk attitude," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Thai leader will travel to Hong Kong on Friday to give a speech aimed at convincing investors and tourists that Thailand is safe again after violent street protests in April.\par \par \uc2 "Thailand is back in business. There are still plenty of opportunities here. We have the key policies in place," the 44-year-old, Oxford-educated premier said.\par \par \uc2 Abhisit said he had not changed his economic forecast made at the time of the April incidents, when he forecast the export-reliant economy could shrink 4-5 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 The government is rolling out a 1.43 trillion baht ($41.4 billion) package of stimulus measures and the programme is expected to run for a three-year period.\par \par \uc2 "If we can fully implement what we have on the table, it should be sufficient," he said, when asked if further measures would be needed.\par \par \uc2 On the baht, Abhisit said: "Our policy is to make sure it is stable and pretty much in line with regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is what has happened."\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to hold down the baht against a weakening U.S. dollar. ($1=34.55 Baht) (Reporting by Darren Schuettler; Editing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ABHISIT/ (INTERVIEW, URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|G|ABX|RNP|DNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090514e55e001nu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
