\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia lifted by dollar profit-taking, won turns up\b0\par\par\uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises as investors take profit from dollar rally\par \par \uc2 * South Korean won turns sharply higher due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Sentiment remains fragile as global recession fear lingers\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 3 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded on Tuesday as investors moved to take profit from the dollar's steep recent rally, with the volatile South Korean won turning sharply higher due to suspected official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> rose as much as 1.5 percent to 1,546.7 per dollar as traders cited official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  propping up the won, which initially lost about 1.5 percent to 1,593.9 per dollar -- a notch above its 11-year trough hit on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, South Korean Finance Minister Yoon Jeung-hyun warned against one-way bets on the won's continued decline, saying the authorities were "resolutely" watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. [ID:nSEO126386]\par \par \uc2 The won, the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia hardest hit by the global financial crisis, is still down about 19 percent against the dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> opened weaker but later drifted higher to 36.09 per dollar, up almost 0.4 percent from Monday's close, as the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars in the market to slow the baht's fall.\par \par \uc2 "The BOT allowed the baht to move lower but later sold dollars to stabilise the baht," said a trader in Bangkok. "They don't want the baht to fall below 36.50 in the short term."\par \par \uc2 The latest development marked a reversal of the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which it had been suspected of buying dollars to deliberately weaken the baht to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have suffered as investors pulled out from the region's stock and bond markets amid the worsening global downturn, which has pinched the region's export-led economies.\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks turned higher as investors started to pick up the beaten-down assets, with the MSCI Asia index of shares outside Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> rising 0.9 percent as of 0618 GMT.\par \par \uc2 "The market was a bit too long dollar/Asia, with most pairs easing lower as U.S. stock futures point to some recovery in equities," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's mostly about risk appetite and positioning rather than fundamental news and I think the firm Asia will only extend if Wall Street rallies."\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Singapore dollar <SGD=> gained half of a percent to 1.5470 per U.S. dollar, coming off its two-year low hit in the previous session, as investors took profit from the U.S. dollar's recent rally.\par \par \uc2 "There has been some profit-taking and the market is also wary of MAS (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) presence as they were rumoured to be selling U.S. dollar," said a trader in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "However, the purchasing managers' index is coming out this afternoon. If the figure is very weak, U.S. dollar will get some support," the trader added.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> opened lower, briefing hitting a 3-year low, but turned higher to 3.709 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz said on Tuesday that the ringgit's weakness with reflected the dollar's broad strength amid the global financial crisis. [ID:nKLA008177]\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> gained half of a percent to 12,090 per dollar, taking its cue from other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0638 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        97.56          97.21       -0.36\par  Sing dlr        1.5477         1.5545       +0.44\par  Taiwan dlr      35.038         35.174       +0.39\par  Korean won     1551.20        1570.30       +1.23\par  Baht             36.07          36.22       +0.42\par  Peso             48.79          49.05       +0.53\par  Rupiah        12090.00       12150.00       +0.50\par  Rupee            51.67          51.90       +0.45\par  Ringgit         3.7030         3.7260       +0.62\par  Yuan            6.8421         6.8458       +0.05\par  Change so far in 2009\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        97.56          90.60       -7.13\par  Sing dlr        1.5477         1.4340       -7.35\par  Taiwan dlr      35.038         32.860       -6.22\par  Korean won     1551.20        1259.50      -18.80\par  Baht             36.07          34.80       -3.52\par  Peso             48.79          47.52       -2.60\par  Rupiah        12090.00       11000.00       -9.00\par  Rupee            51.67          48.71       -5.73\par  Ringgit         3.7030         3.4500       -6.83\par  Yuan            6.8421         6.8230       -0.28\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Jan Dahinten)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MAR