\par\par \b \uc2 Aberdeen New Thai IT Interim Results\b0\par\par\uc2 6,307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2008\par 15:20\par \uc2 Regulatory News Service\par \uc2 RNS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par \par TIDMANW \par  \par RNS Number : 3519G \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aberdeen New Thai Inv Trust PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par 21 October 2008 \par  \par ? \par ABERDEEN NEW THAI INVESTMENT TRUST PLC \par  \par  \par HALF-YEARLY FINANCIAL REPORT FOR THE SIX MONTHS ENDED 31 AUGUST 2008 \par  \par  \par The following is the unaudited Interim Board Report for the six months ended 31 \par August 2008. \par  \par  \par Interim Board Report \par  \par  \par Investment Objective \par The investment objective of the Company is to provide Shareholders with a high \par degree of long-term, above average capital growth through investment in \par Thailand. \par  \par  \par Investment Policy \par The Company's assets are invested in a diversified portfolio of securities \par (substantially in the form of equities or equity related securities such as \par convertible securities and warrants) in companies spread across a range of \par industries which are quoted on the Stock Exchange of Thailand ('SET'). \par  \par  \par Background \par Despite the tumultuous political backdrop and turbulent stock market, your \par Company delivered a satisfactory performance relative to its benchmark for the \par half-year under review. The capital net asset value per share of your Company's \par portfolio fell by 12.1% to 172.4p while the discount widened in the period \par resulting in a fall of 21.7% in the share price to 146.5p. This compares with a \par fall in the Company's benchmark, the Stock Exchange of Thailand ("SET") Index, \par of 17.1% in Sterling terms, on a like-for-like basis. The weak performance of \par the PTT group companies (with the exception of the PTTEP holding) and the \par property and construction sectors were largely responsible for the Index's poor \par performance. \par  \par  \par Overview \par Events that transpired during the review period, both globally and in Thailand \par itself have been nothing short of dramatic. \par  \par  \par Thai equities fell sharply due to escalating domestic political tensions and \par fears of slowing economic growth owing to the global financial crisis. Easing \par inflation pressure and better-than-expected second-quarter US economic data \par towards the end of the review period failed to lift poor investor sentiment. \par  \par  \par The beleaguered coalition government, led by (former Prime Minister) \par Thaksin-friendly members of the People's Power Party (PPP), came under \par increasing pressure during the period. Political intrigue surrounded the \par dismissal of several cabinet ministers, on vote-buying charges and border \par disputes with Cambodia. To add to the turmoil, Thaksin returned to Thailand to \par face corruption charges, but soon fled. Hopes that political tensions would ease \par following this "second escape" turned out to be as ephemeral as his visit. \par Political instability culminated in protestors from the People's Alliance for \par Democracy (PAD) storming Government House, forcing Prime Minister Samak's \par resignation. \par  \par  \par The impact of the unrest on the economy was greatly felt and consumer confidence \par weakened considerably. \par  \par  \par To aggravate the situation, annual consumer price inflation reached a \par decade-high of 9.2% in July. The marked slowdown in second-quarter GDP growth \par caused first-half expansion to register 5.7%. This led to a downgrade in the \par growth forecast for the calendar year to 4.8%-5.8%, while the annual inflation \par target was increased to 5.0%-5.5%. Despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \par the baht, which had earlier strengthened, came under pressure as foreign \par short-term capital flows turned negative. Nevertheless, the weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \par rising agricultural prices bolstered exports, which remained the main driver of \par the economy. Second-quarter export growth was a robust 26% year-on-year. \par  \par  \par Policymakers have tried to curb inflationary pressures by raising interest rates \par from 3.25% to 3.75%. The Samak administration introduced transport subsidies and \par price caps on certain utilities which will remain in force until the year-end. \par It also unveiled several stimulus packages targeted at conserving energy and \par reviving investment. Given the precarious position of the current \par administration, the outcome of these measures remains unclear. \par  \par  \par Portfolio \par The capital net asset value of your Company fell 12.1% during the period under \par review, outperforming the benchmark's decline of 17.1% in capital terms. The \par out-performance was largely due to market rotation into more defensive \par companies, many of which are held in your Company's portfolio. From a sector \par perspective, the underweight position in resources and overweight in financials \par also helped performance. Overall, the businesses of your Company's investments \par are faring well in a tough environment, a general reflection of focused, \par superior management and strong balance sheets. \par  \par  \par Despite the deterioration in consumer confidence, your Company's \par consumer-related stocks outperformed the benchmark, with Big C Supercenter and \par Alucon leading gains. Alucon reported strong results for the six months to June, \par supported by resilient aerosol can demand and improved efficiency while Big C \par Supercenter also posted solid sales. The supermarket, owned by French retailer \par Casino Group, is still benefiting from its first mover advantage into \par hypermarkets, having focused on the upcountry markets. Its strong brand name and \par customer loyalty remain key competitive advantages. Its main rival, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siam Makro\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \par another of your Company's holdings, has a slight advantage over foreign-owned \par Big C Supercenter because of the government's protectionist regulations but both \par are top quality companies that are seeing steady store expansion. \par  \par  \par Among financials, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thai Reinsurance\f28 \uc2 \f2  enjoys a solid position as the sole domestic \par reinsurer. Its main appeal lies in its long-standing track record and \par conservative investment strategy. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siam Commercial Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , one of the stronger \par banks in the country, has been able to grow assets at a high rate without \par deterioration in asset quality. It is also backed by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Crown Property Bureau\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \par which manages the wealth of the royal family. Other stocks which outperformed \par the benchmark include recent addition \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bumrungrad Hospital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The company's \par business model for medical tourism is compelling and it is able to offer a high \par quality service at attractive prices. It has also expanded into overseas markets \par such as those in the Middle East, having teamed up with solid local partners. \par  \par  \par In the information technology sector, Hana Microelectronics performed well \par backed by a solid balance sheet, decent cash flows and high dividend payout. \par Energy-heavyweight PTTEP is the Company's largest holding. The oil and gas \par company fell only 5.4%, supported by increasing volumes from recently discovered \par reserves and it is able to sell to the domestic market at international, rather \par than controlled, prices. Its share price outperformed strongly compared with its \par sister companies, which saw their margins squeezed during the review period. \par  \par  \par Your Manager continues to be comfortable not holding index heavyweights such as \par Bangkok Bank, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Total Access Communication\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Airports of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Although the \par former two performed roughly in line with the benchmark, the share price of the \par government-linked \f28 \uc2 \f2 Airports of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell sharply. \par  \par  \par On the negative side, holdings that cost the Company include Muang Thai \par Insurance, formerly Phatra Insurance, and now part of the Kasikorn Group. The \par stock's decline was greater than the stock market's fall as first-half earnings \par fell 75%. Tisco Bank also lagged the market, falling sharply, but the company \par has a highly professional management team that has made a commitment to higher \par dividend payouts. \par  \par  \par It should be noted that, although the Company is overweight financials, it is in \par fact underweight the bank sector. The overweight reflects positions in the \par insurance and finance company sectors. Nevertheless, Thai banks generally, and \par our holdings in particular, have no or very limited exposure to US \par mortgage-related credit derivatives. \par  \par  \par Property developer Central Pattana was hurt by the weak property market, but the \par extension of its land lease contract with Lad Prao shopping mall for another 20 \par years, pending cabinet approval, should provide support for the stock. Higher \par oil prices and lower bunkering charges weighed on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Regional Container Lines\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par Elsewhere, political uncertainty and reduced government spending adversely \par affected \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siam City Cement\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par  \par  \par Your Manager is a long-term fundamental investor and as a result, portfolio \par activity was fairly limited. We added to our positions in Thai Stanley, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siam \par City Cement\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bumrungrad Hospital\f28 \uc2 \f2  while the position in Eastern Water \par Resources Development was reduced owing to changes in its business model. We \par also engaged in some profit-taking in PTTEP following the rally in its share \par price. \par  \par Outlook \par The volatility in the SET Index, which took its cue from political upheavals and \par events unfolding in global financial markets, looks likely to persist in the \par medium-term. Recently, the Thai courts found Prime Minister Samak guilty of a \par constitutional breach and ordered him to resign. Deputy Prime Minister Somchai \par Wongsawat, a brother-in-law of former Premier Thaksin, was chosen as the new \par leader, a move that has enraged Thaksin opponents. The political impasse between \par the PPP and the PAD is unlikely to be resolved while the PPP remains in power. \par  \par  \par If the political crisis persists over an extended period, the economic costs \par could be high. The country is already feeling the effects of the global economic \par slowdown. The current account deficit could widen further if slowing export \par growth is hit by further disruptions from anti-government protestors. The \par tourism sector is also at risk of being severely affected, as neighbouring \par countries warned their citizens to avoid non-essential travel to the Kingdom. \par  \par  \par The baht is also likely to remain under pressure. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has depreciated \par significantly year to date, especially after the state of emergency was \par declared. Despite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's reassurance that it will keep the \par volatility of the baht in check, the outlook remains bleak. Monetary policy has \par not been adequate to quell inflation expectations and the persistent rise in \par prices has taken a toll on exchange rate expectations. Adding to the gloom is \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 21-10-08 1320GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the deteriorating external account. \par  \par  \par So far, local businesses have been relatively unaffected by the domestic drama. \par Over the years, they have grown accustomed to political disruptions and have not \par allowed them to materially affect their business investment decisions. Given the \par external environment, however, we expect corporate earnings to flatten or even \par decline in the next 12 to 18 months, while dividend payouts are likely to follow \par the same trajectory. On a positive note, current valuations are now more \par reasonable, with your portfolio trading on a multiple of around 12 times 2008 \par earnings, versus 14 times a year ago. While the next twelve months are likely to \par be volatile, valuations on a five-year basis are attractive. \par  \par  \par Risks and Uncertainties \par The following risks are the principal risks which are considered by the \par Directors to be material to Shareholders. \par  \par  \par Ordinary shares: The market price and the realisable value of the Ordinary \par shares, as well as being affected by their underlying net asset value, also take \par into account supply and demand for the Ordinary shares, market conditions and \par general investor sentiment. As such, the market value and the realisable value \par of the Ordinary shares may fluctuate and vary considerably from the net asset \par value of the Ordinary shares and investors may not be able to realise the value \par of their original investment. \par  \par  \par Dividends: The Company will only pay dividends on the Ordinary shares to the \par extent that it has profits available for that purpose. The ability of the \par Company to pay any dividends in respect of the Ordinary shares and any future \par dividend growth will depend primarily on the level of income received from its \par investments. Accordingly, the amount of the dividends paid to Shareholders may \par fluctuate. \par  \par  \par Borrowings: Whilst the use of borrowings should enhance the total return on the \par Ordinary shares where the return on the Company's underlying assets is rising \par and exceeds the cost of borrowing, it will have the opposite effect where the \par underlying return is less than the cost of borrowing, further reducing the total \par return on the Ordinary shares. As at 31 August 2008, the Company had gearing, \par net of cash balances, equivalent to 7.0% of shareholders' funds and the \par Directors keep this level of gearing under regular review. \par  \par  \par Market risks: The Company's investments are subject to normal market \par fluctuations and the risks inherent in the purchase, holding or selling of \par securities, and there can be no assurance that appreciation in the value of \par those investments will occur. Investment in Thailand involves a greater degree \par of risk than that usually associated with investment in more developed \par securities markets. The Directors continue to monitor regularly the level of \par counter-party risk faced by the Company. \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks: The Company accounts for its activities and reports its \par results in Sterling while investments are made and realised in Thai Baht. It is \par not the Company's present intention to engage in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedging, although it \par reserves the right to do so. Accordingly, the movement of exchange rates between \par Sterling and the other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in which the Company's investments are \par denominated, or its borrowings are drawn down in, may have a material effect, \par unfavourable as well as favourable, on the returns otherwise experienced on the \par investments made by the Company. \par  \par  \par Taxation and exchange controls: Any change in the Company's tax status or in \par taxation legislation (including the tax treatment of dividends or other \par investment income received by the Company) could affect the value of the \par investments held by the Company, affect the Company's ability to provide returns \par to Shareholders or alter the post-tax returns to Shareholders. \par  \par  \par The Company may purchase investments that may be subject to exchange controls or \par withholding taxes in various jurisdictions. In the event that exchange controls \par or withholding taxes are imposed with respect to any of the Company's \par investments, the effect will generally be to reduce the income received by the \par Company on its investments and the capital value of the affected investments. \par  \par  \par General: The Company does not have a fixed winding-up date and, therefore, \par unless Shareholders vote to wind up the Company, Shareholders will only be able \par to realise their investment through the market. \par  \par  \par Related Parties \par Mr Hugh Young, a director of the Company, is also a director of the investment \par manager, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aberdeen Asset Management Asia Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Mr Peter Bristowe, a director \par of the Company, is also a director and shareholder of Edison Investment \par Management Research Limited which supplies investor relations and equity \par research services to the Company to the value of GBP10,000 per annum which the \par other members of the Board consider to be immaterial. \par  \par Directors' Responsibility Statement \par The Directors are responsible for preparing the Half-Yearly Financial Report in \par accordance with applicable law and regulations. The Directors confirm that to \par the best of their knowledge: \par   *  the Interim Financial Statements have been prepared in accordance with the \par   Accounting Standards Board's statement "Half-Yearly Financial Reports"; and \par   *  the Interim Board Report includes a fair review of the information required by \par   4.2.7R and 4.2.8R of the FSA's Disclosure and Transparency Rules. \par  \par