\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thai Aug export growth slows, consumption falls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kitiphong Thaichareon   \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2008\par 04:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds economists' comments, details)\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Thailand's annual export growth fell sharply in August, keeping its trade and current accounts in deficit for a second straight month, and economists said that trend would continue for the rest of 2008 at least.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Thailand also said on Tuesday that consumption dropped in August from July, and analysts said the outlook was worrying, with global financial problems coming on top of domestic political unrest.\par \par \uc2 The value of exports in August, held back by easing rice and commodity prices, rose just 15.5 percent from a year earlier, the smallest rise in 11 months after 43.9 percent in July and 28.5 percent in June.\par \par \uc2 Growth in August, which was in line with the 15.0 percent expected by analysts, fell from the previous month due to slower shipments to the United States, China and other Asian neighbours, early customs data showed.\par \par \uc2 "Most of the August figures show clear signs of weakness compared to a month earlier, especially export growth," economist Pimonwan Mahujchariyawong of Kasikorn Research Center said.\par \par \uc2 "But this is just the beginning and we will see a more severe impact in the following months until the rest of this year," she added.\par \par \uc2 Rice exports from Thailand, the world's biggest exporter, rose 120 percent in value in August from a year before, but that was down from a 212 percent rise in July, customs data showed.\par \par \uc2 Total imports rose 26.9 percent in August from a year earlier to $16.46 billion after a 53.4 percent rise to a record $17.55 billion in July. The August trade deficit narrowed to a lower than expected $675 million versus $762 million a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 Economists had forecast a $788 million deficit.\par \par \uc2 The current account deficit also narrowed to $853 million from an upwardly revised $906 million in July. SUSTAINED DEFICIT\par \par \uc2 "We expect the current account deficit to continue for the remaining months of this year because of slowing exports and declining tourism revenues, which would weaken the baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the rest of this year," said economist Usara Wilaipich of Standard Chartered Bank.\par \par \uc2 The baht traded around 33.92 per dollar on Tuesday after the data versus 33.95 at midday and 34.04 late on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Tarisa Watanagase said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday and traders said it had bought the baht to stop it weakening too quickly as investors' aversion to risk grew.\par \par \uc2 The official data showed that private investment rose a seasonally adjusted 0.7 percent in August, the same pace seen in July. But annual growth slowed to 3.8 percent from 4.4 percent a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 Consumption slowed, falling a seasonally adjusted 0.6 percent in August after a 3.4 percent rise a month earlier, with annual growth easing to 7.2 percent from 9.3 percent in July.\par \par \uc2 Pimonwan at Kasikorn Research said the rest of the year looked even more worrying for consumption.\par \par \uc2 "Falling inflation due to weaker oil is a plus, but the market has factored that in for quite some time now. The external impact from financial problems will make it tougher for the domestic economy to regain strength," she said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters have forecast that September consumer price data on Wednesday would show inflation easing to 6.2 percent from 6.4 percent in August, restrained by petrol price subsidies and state measures to cut utility and transport charges. (Additional reporting by Bangkok newsroom; Writing by Vithoon Amorn; Editing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|ABX|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080930e49u0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
