\par\par \b \uc2 AT A GLANCE: Thai State Of Emergency Rattles Regional Mkts\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2008\par 13:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Thailand declared a state of emergency in Bangkok Tuesday to clamp down on anti-government protests that erupted into deadly clashes overnight, with the army ordered to quell any further clashes. Thai shares and the baht fell on the news. Following are Dow Jones top stories so far:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =4TH UPDATE: THAI PM IMPOSES EMERGENCY; ARMY WON'T EVICT MOB \f28 \par \par \uc2 Thailand's beleaguered Prime Minister Samak Sundaravej handed over his country's capital to the powerful military Tuesday, in what appeared to be a desperate attempt to cling onto power after deadly clashes between his supporters and antigovernment demonstrators. (published 1008 GMT)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: THAI STATE OF EMERGENCY CRIMPS SOUTHEAST ASIAN MKTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Thai state of emergency sent the baht lower and weighed on Southeast Asia markets Tuesday, though analysts doubted the contagion would last long. (published 0336 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI ARMY CHIEF: COUP IS NOT SOLUTION TO POLITICAL PROBLEMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's army chief Anupong Paochinda said Tuesday that a coup isn't a solution to the country's political problems. (published 0733 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: THAI VOTE AGENCY RECOMMENDS RULING PARTY DISSOLUTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's Election Commission on Tuesday recommended the ruling People Power Party be dissolved for vote fraud, piling more pressure on an embattled government that just hours earlier declared a state of emergency in Bangkok following deadly protests. (published 0634 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: THREE AIRLINES CANCEL BANGKOK-HAT YAI FLIGHTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Three airlines canceled a total of 16 flights between Bangkok and the commercial city of Hat Yai in southern Thailand on Tuesday afternoon after several hundred antigovernment protesters massed at the southern airport. (published 0753 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS:FUND MANAGERS SEE TEMPORARY MKT DIP FROM THAI UPHEAVAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's declared state of emergency could temporarily accelerate an investor pullout that began in the wake of a coup two years ago but outflows will likely be contained, fund managers said. (published 0627 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: THAI PM'S DAYS IN POWER LOOK NUMBERED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Samak Sundaravej's days as Thai prime minister look to be numbered after pro-government militants clashed with anti-government protesters outside his office in Bangkok early Tuesday and with a large nationwide strike planned for Wednesday. (published 0602 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  CONFIRMS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENED\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday it \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market this morning to stem the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's slide after the government declared a state of emergency. (published 0551 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:UK, AUSTRALIA, ASIAN NATIONS ISSUE THAI TRAVEL WARNINGS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's key tourism industry faces a wave of cancelations as Australia, the U.K., and several Asian nations told citizens to be vigilant or possibly avoid the country outright after deadly clashes and a lockdown in the capital, Bangkok. (published 0559 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SOME 500 THAI PROTESTERS AT HAT YAI AIRPORT, DEMAND CLOSURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some 500 antigovernment protesters have massed at the entrance of Hat Yai airport, which serves an important commercial city in southern Thailand, demanding the airport's closure. (published 0547 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI ARMY CHIEF: NOT ARMY'S ROLE TO EVICT GOVT HOUSE PROTESTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's army chief Anupong Paochinda said Tuesday it's not the army's role to evict antigovernment protesters occupying Government House in Bangkok. (published 0728 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: POLITICS ARE BACK, DOGGING SOUTHEAST ASIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Street protests in Thailand have stirred concern about political turmoil elsewhere in Southeast Asia, a worry that could keep the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  under pressure in coming days. (published 0505 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS: THAI ECONOMY UNDER SIEGE AS POLITICAL CRISIS WORSENS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The escalating political crisis now engulfing Thailand will take a heavy toll on the country's economy if it's prolonged, adding to the burden of unfavorable global economic conditions. (published 1032 GMT) [ 02-09-08 1109GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080902e492000in\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
