\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Thai emergency hits baht and shares, but bonds rise\b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2008\par 06:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stocks fall 2.3 percent, hit lowest since Jan 2007\par \par \uc2 * Baht hits one-year low, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps in\par \par \uc2 * Government bonds seen as safe haven with inflation peaking (Updates with closing stock market prices)\par \par \uc2 By Orathai Sriring\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Sept 2 (Reuters) - Thai stocks hit a 19-month low and the baht touched a one-year trough on Tuesday after the prime minister imposed emergency rule, but domestic government bonds rose as local investors sought safe haven for their funds.\par \par \uc2 Shares in the tourist sector took a hit after foreigners cancelled flights when some countries issued travel warnings, although \uc2 Thai Airways\uc2  rallied in late trade.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark SET index <.SETI> ended down 2.3 percent at 659.51 after touching 655.62, its lowest since January 2007. The market has lost 24.7 percent since street protests began on May 25.\par \par \uc2 Thai Prime Minister Samak Sundaravej declared a state of emergency in Bangkok on Tuesday and gave the army control of public order after a man died in overnight clashes between pro- and anti-government protesters. [ID:nSP172153].\par \par \uc2 "Announcing a state of emergency is proper and timely. But if you ask me if this is the beginning of the end? No, it isn't," said Puwadol Lapudomsuk, a senior strategist at Asia Plus Securities, adding he expected a gradual fall, not panic selling.\par \par \uc2 Kosin Sripaiboon at UOB KayHian Securities felt the same.\par \par \uc2 "Selling will be coming from both sides, foreign and local investors, but I don't really think panic selling will be as bad as when the former government imposed a 30 percent capital control measure," he said, referring to the record one-day fall of 14.8 percent in reaction to capital controls in December 2006.\par \par \uc2 Bourse chief Patareeya Benjapolchai remained calm. "The circuit breaker system will work automatically if the market falls sharply to a certain point but right now everything is manageable," he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=TH> was weaker, in line with other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at 34.49 per dollar in late trade after hitting a one-year low of 34.52.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The baht had weakened rapidly this morning, so we had to take care of it. If it is volatile, we will step in," Deputy Governor Atchana Waiquamdee told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Investors in Thailand's dollar-denominated bonds have taken fright at the political crisis, with anti-government supporters occupying the prime minister's official compound, but domestic bonds continued their recent strong run.\par \par \uc2 "Political noise reduces the attractiveness of risky alternatives to safe-haven government bonds," ING analyst Tim Condon said in a report.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year domestic bond yield <TH10YT=RR> shed 5 basis points to 4.45 percent bid. It has fallen around 180 bps from a June peak as investors took the view that both inflation and interest rates have peaked.\par \par \uc2 But five-year credit default swaps (CDS) <THAILD5UA=GFI> -- the cost of protecting against a default in Thailand's dollar-denominated debt -- widened by as much as 10 bps to 150.\par \par \uc2 The CDS later tightened to 146 bps, a Hong Kong trader said, meaning it would cost investors $146,000 to protect against $10 million of sovereign debt annually for five years.\par \par \uc2 On the stock market, shares in national carrier \uc2 Thai Airways\uc2  bounced back to end 4.4 percent higher at 14.30 baht after hitting a record low of 13.30 baht.\par \par \uc2 The national carrier's president said passengers were cancelling due to the unrest, but the share was helped by late bargain-hunting and another drop in fuel prices, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Airports of Thailand\uc2  was down 1.3 percent at 37 baht. Several airports were closed by protests at the end of last week but have now reopened.\par \par \uc2 Among big losers, top energy firm PTT fell 4.6 percent and market leader Bangkok Bank dropped 3.4 percent. LINKS > More stories on Thailand's political turmoil [ID:nBKK69595] ($1=34.43 Baht) (Additional reporting by Rafael Nam in Hong Kong, Kevin Yao in Singapore, Ploy Chitsomboon and Viparat Jantraprap in Bangkok; Editing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-PROTEST/MARKETS (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|SI|ABX|RNP|DNP|PCM|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080902e4920007m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
