\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 969 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2008\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =3RD UPDATE: THAI PM IMPOSES EMERGENCY; PROTESTERS DIG IN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's embattled Prime Minister Samak Sundaravej declared a state of emergency in Bangkok on Tuesday, handing control of the capital to the army after violent clashes between his supporters and antigovernment protesters left one dead and dozens injured. (published 0502 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AT A GLANCE: THAI STATE OF EMERGENCY RATTLES REGIONAL MKTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand declared a state of emergency in Bangkok Tuesday to clamp down on anti-government protests that erupted into deadly clashes overnight, with the army ordered to quell any further clashes. (published 0433 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIA RBA CAUTIOUSLY CUTS RATES; EYES DEMAND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of Australia Tuesday cut its cash rate target for the first time in seven years to avert an economic hard landing but signaled it would be cautious in easing policy further. (published 0502 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN'S RULING PARTY LEADER ASO MAY SEEK PM POST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso, the secretary-general of Japan's ruling party, signaled on Tuesday he would run for prime minister, saying he believes he is qualified to take over from outgoing Yasuo Fukuda. (published 0247 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  CONFIRMS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENED\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday it \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market this morning to stem the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's slide after the government declared a state of emergency. (published 0551 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:JAPAN IBUKI SEES QUICK MOVE BY NEW GOVT ON ECON STEPS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A day after Japanese Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda's surprise resignation, Finance Minister Bunmei Ibuki tried to reassure the public Tuesday, saying whoever takes over will quickly move on planned economic stimulus steps. (published 0454 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN YOSANO: NEXT PM SHOULD TAKE ACTION FOR ECON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Economy Minister Kaoru Yosano called on the next prime minister to continue taking action over the flagging economy and to compile an extra budget for this fiscal year. (published 0223 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: BOJ SHIRAKAWA KEEPS WEAK VIEWS ON JAPAN, GLOBAL ECON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's economy may remain sluggish for the time being but will probably avoid a serious downturn, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's governor said Tuesday. (published 0304 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN REAL GDP GREW 0.7% IN JULY ON EXPORTS REBOUND -NIKKEI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Real gross domestic product inched up 0.7% on the month in July, rising for the first time in two months thanks to a recovery in exports, according to data released Monday by the Japan Center for Economic Research, the Nikkei reported in its Tuesday morning edition. (published 2259 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVT: WILL ACT IF DOLLAR SURGES AGAINST THE WON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's government vowed Tuesday to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if the dollar surges against the won, reiterating a message that has now become almost a daily ritual. (published 0054 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: S KOREA GOVT:HAS ENOUGH DLRS TO ACT VS ANY FINCL RISK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior official at South Korea's finance ministry said Tuesday that the nation has enough dollars in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, denying any possible impact from the so-called September crisis that some believe will destabilize the local financial system. (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOK: DOLLAR STRENGTH VS WON UNLIKELY TO VANISH SOON - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Korea Governor Lee Seongtae said the recent strength of the dollar against the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "will continue for a considerable period of time," Yonhap News Agency reported Tuesday. (published 0120 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA BANK REGULATOR DRAFTS RULES TO CURB LOAN MISUSE -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's banking regulator is drafting rules to prevent bank clients from misusing loans, a senior official at a local financial institution said Tuesday. (published 0307 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIA JULY BUILDING APPROVALS WEAK AS RATES BITE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian building approvals weakened in July, adding to evidence of a sharp downturn in the housing sector and bolstering the case for an immediate cut in official interest rates. (published 0238 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN: SLOWING GROWTH TO PUSH UP UNEMPLOYMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan said Tuesday that slowing economic growth will lead to lower employment growth and higher unemployment levels. (published 2259 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RESERVES $243.20B AS OF END-AUG; $247.52B END-JUL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves shrank to their lowest in 18 months in August, following suspected dollar-selling by the authorities to support a sliding won. (published 2100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: POLITICS ARE BACK, DOGGING SOUTHEAST ASIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Street protests in Thailand have stirred concern about political turmoil elsewhere in Southeast Asia, a worry that could keep the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  under pressure in coming days. (published 0505 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par \par =MONEY TALKS: JAPAN PM'S EXIT MAY PROMPT SHAKEUP - SOME DAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two years, two Japanese prime ministers. (published 0441 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: WILL THE POUND STILL BUY YOU A BEER IN ASIA? \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We all know the British pound is in the doghouse when it comes to the U.S. dollar and the euro. (published 0033 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: PHILIPPINE AUG CPI LIKELY +12.5%; 17-YR HIGH - POLL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in the Philippines likely surged to a fresh 17-year high in August, bolstered by higher prices of food and other commodities due to supply bottlenecks caused by typhoons and a recent wage increase. (published 0420 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJA(9/2) ZARDARI WOULD HOLD BROAD PAKISTAN POWER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asif Ali Zardari's party recently promised to curtail the powers of the next president of Pakistan after years of Pervez Musharraf's strongman rule. (published 2130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJA(9/2) SUBBARAO WILL TAKE HELM AT INDIA'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian government named Finance Secretary Duvvuri Subbarao, the finance ministry's top-ranking bureaucrat, as its new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor in a move that is expected to continue the bank's strict anti-inflation policies. (published 2130 GMT) [ 02-09-08 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080902e49200051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
