\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia rises on Fed rate view, mood cautious\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2008\par 05:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 26 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Thursday after the Federal Reserve held interest rates steady and dampened expectations for an imminent rate rise, but buying interest remained fragile after recent sell-off.\par \par \uc2 The Fed held interest rates at 2 percent on Wednesday, as widely expected, and signalled it was no rush to raise them, even as it voiced greater concerns about inflation. [ID:nN25433059]\par \par \uc2 Reduced prospects of a U.S. rate rise weighed on the dollar and made higher-yielding emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more appealing.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> rose as far as 44.40 per dollar, up about 0.4 percent from Wednesday's close.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> rose as far as 1.3643 per U.S. dollar, up a fifth of a percent from late Asian trade on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 A Singapore-based trader said the view that the Fed was in no rush to raise rates prodded investors to buy the Singapore dollar, which is expected to trade between 1.3630 and 1.3720 for the day.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> rose to 3.2520 per dollar, up more than 0.3 percent from late Asian trade on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The risk appetite has picked up -- note rallies in stocks, tighter spreads, lower volatility and a generally softer U.S. dollar," said Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI index of stocks in the Asia-Pacific region excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> edged up about 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Still, investors remain cautious after the recent selling of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  spurred by foreign sales of regional stocks amid inflation worries stoked by record high oil prices, and a broad rise in the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not breaking ranges yet, with oil still weighing and inflation concerns not fading," said Han Sia Yeo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Bank of America.\par \par \uc2 BULLISH ON TAIWAN DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> rose a fifth of a percent to 33.50 per dollar as traders cited dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BoT) to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "They sold (dollars) from 33.60 to 33.55," said one Bangkok-based trader.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in South Korea, Thailand, the Philippines and India have in recent week sold dollars to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of fear that their weakness would stoke inflation.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> rose a quarter of a percent to a one-week high at 30.30 per U.S. dollar, as the market expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates by at least 12.5 basis points in its drawn out campaign to curb inflation. [ID:nHKG261850]\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=>, the region's worst performer, briefly rose to 1,033.9 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Westpac\uc2  strategist Sean Callow suggested investors should go short on the won against the dollar via non-deliverable forwards.\par \par \uc2 "The Taiwan dollar is more resilient, backed by stronger current account position and far less gloomy foreign sentiment over Taiwan stocks than Korean stocks," he said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> hit a post-revaluation high at 8.8612 per dollar as policy makers are seen still favouring the steady rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help quell inflation.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has gained 6.4 percent so far this year, trailing the Taiwan dollar, which has gained 6.9 percent. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0800 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       108.07         107.93       -0.13\par  Sing dlr        1.3655         1.3669       +0.10\par  Taiwan dlr      30.373         30.380       +0.02\par  Korean won     1037.50        1037.80       +0.03\par  Baht             33.55          33.57       +0.06\par  Peso             44.45          44.59       +0.31\par  Rupiah         9225.00        9243.00       +0.20\par  Rupee            42.69          42.73       +0.11\par  Ringgit         3.2550         3.2635       +0.26\par  Yuan            6.8628         6.8653       +0.04\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       108.07         111.33       +3.02\par  Sing dlr        1.3655         1.4382       +5.32\par  Taiwan dlr      30.373         32.443       +6.82\par  Korean won     1037.50         935.70       -9.81\par  Baht             33.55          33.68       +0.39\par  Peso             44.45          41.28       -7.13\par  Rupiah         9225.00        9390.00       +1.79\par  Rupee            42.69          39.41       -7.67\par  Ringgit         3.2550         3.3050       +1.54\par  Yuan            6.8628         7.3041       +6.43\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080629e46q0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pic