\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thai baht hit by political tensions -  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2008\par 08:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, June 20 (Reuters) - Rising political tensions are causing the baht to fall too quickly, although there is no panic in the market, Bank of Thailand Assistant Governor Suchada Kirakul said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 She said that the fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being prompted by capital outflows as foreign investors sell Thai shares.\par \par \uc2 "It is weakening too quickly, but there is no panic in the market yet. Exporters are still selling dollars and importers are buying," she told reporters.\par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=TH> was trading around 33.43 to the dollar at 0830 GMT. It dropped as far as 33.55 earlier, its lowest level in more than five months, as a major anti-government protest got underway in Bangkok.\par \par \uc2 "We are taking care of it as usual, but we don't have to do it too much at the moment," she said, using words that usually indicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars to defend the baht regularly in the past few weeks. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by $4 billion in the week to June 13, suggesting reserves had been used to prop up the baht.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of protesters have laid siege to the government's headquarters in an attempt to force the five-month-old administration from power. [ID:nBKK283007].\par \par \uc2 Protestors view the coalition government elected in December as an illegitimate proxy of former Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra.\par \par \uc2 The stock market <.SETI> has shed 15 percent since the anti-government protests began on May 25, with net foreign selling of 39 billion baht ($1.2 billion) worth of Thai shares.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  meanwhile, has fallen close to 5 percent against the dollar over the same period.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Boontiwa Wichakul; Writing by Orathai Sriring; Editing by Darren Schuettler)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/BAHT (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|ABX|BNX|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080620e46k000j5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
