\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW-Thai cenbank in market to stem baht fall\b0\par\par\uc2 By Orathai Sriring   \par \uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2008\par 14:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, June 6 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BoT) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Friday to stem a sharp fall in the baht, and is ready to prevent any future excessive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Assistant Governor Suchada Kirakula said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The baht really weakened too fast today, but don't panic," Suchada told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "You have to believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking care (of the baht) to prevent its volatility," she said, adding she expected the baht weakness to slow on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=TH> hit 33.10/18 to the dollar on Friday, more than a 4-month low and down from 32.87/89 on Thursday. However, it has still risen 1.7 percent against the dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Suchada said the baht's recent weakness was due to dollar buying by oil importers and equities-linked capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have sold 26 billion baht ($785 million) net of Thai shares so far this year due to concerns about renewed political conflict and rising inflation. They bought 55 billion net of Thai stocks in 2007.\par \par \uc2 Anti-government protests on the streets of Bangkok worried investors about a repeat of the September 2006 military coup that ousted Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra.\par \par \uc2 Suchada suggested exporters and importers should hedge and not focus on specific baht levels.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's economic fundamentals were still strong despite soaring inflation and renewed political uncertainty, she said, pointing to $2 billion in foreign direct investment in the first quarter of 2008.\par \par \uc2 "Our economic fundamentals are not bad," she said.\par \par \uc2 Suchada said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue its current inflation-targeting policy.\par \par \uc2 Annual headline inflation surged to near a 10-year high of 7.6 percent in May, up from 6.2 percent in April, raising expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise its policy rate later this year after keeping it at 3.25 percent since July last year.\par \par \uc2 On establishing a sovereign wealth fund, Suchada said the Finance Ministry was studying the plan, but she did not expect that in the short term.\par \par \uc2 "It's probably not a hot issue now," she said without elaborating, citing the weaker baht.\par \par \uc2 Funds are popular among other emerging countries seeking ways to increase returns in growing foreign reserves. Thailand had $108.9 billion in foreign reserves and $18.8 billion in net forward positions as of May 30. ($1=33.10 Baht) (Editing by George Obulutsa and Gerrard Raven)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|ABX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080609e4660013s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
