\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Thai  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 : Baht Quite Stable, Less Need To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 156 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2008\par 06:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Tarisa Watanagase said Tuesday that the baht is quite stable currently and there is less need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 'take care' of the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The baht's recent movement was driven mainly by international trade flows and external factors, and hardly by any domestic factors, Tarisa told reporters on the sidelines of an investment forum.\par \par \uc2 The baht has weakened against the dollar in the past few weeks, partly as Thailand's trade surplus has narrowed from last year's high level.\par \par \uc2 Tarisa said the bank is closely monitoring the baht to ensure that it moves in line with market mechanisms and is ready to take action against any speculation on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . -By Bangkok Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020080513e45d00336\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
