\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Korea won leads Asian rebound ahead of Fed   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 862 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 04:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 18 (Reuters) - The South Korean won jumped on Tuesday amid suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to prop up the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leading a rebound among Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of an expected Fed interest rate cut later in the day.   \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> rose as far as 1,008.9 per dollar, up almost 2 percent from late Asian trade on Monday, as dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities may have sold some $1 billion during the morning session to support the won.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier, Deputy Finance Minister Shin Je-yoon warned against the won's sharp falls versus the dollar and vowed to take measures to smooth its volatility.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled just over 3 percent in the previous session, as investors sold Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on fears of the spreading credit crisis after news of the sale of investment bank  \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2  and the Fed's emergency discount-rate cut.   \par \par \uc2 "We still see negative risks for the won," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank.   \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. credit crisis is playing a role in the funding problems in Korea," he said, expecting the won to fall further to 1,032 in the near term.   \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> briefly hit 9,220 per dollar, up nearly 0.9 percent from late Asian trade on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> rose as far as 1.3785 per U.S. dollar, up nearly half of a percent from late Asian trade on Monday, as investors positioned for a hefty interest rate cut by the Federal Reserve later in the day.   \par \par \uc2 "The market expects a rate cut between 0.75 and 1 percentage point, and it's most likely they have to give in to market demand," said a trader in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed on Sunday announced emergency measures to stem a global credit crisis, including cutting its discount rate to 3.25 percent from 3.5 percent and approving special financing to help  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  acquire the beleaguered  \uc2 Bear Stearns.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is widely expected to slash its benchmark interest rate, currently at 3 percent, at its policy meeting on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 WIDENING RATE GAPS   \par \par \uc2 The move would further widen the interest rate differentials between the United States and Asian countries, which could in turn help spur capital inflows into the region, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Policy interest rates in emerging Asian countries range from 3.25 percent in Thailand to 8 percent in Indonesia.   \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> gained 0.8 percent to 31.13 per dollar, its highest since August 1997, as exporters and investors sold the dollar in anticipation of the Fed's rate cut.   \par \par \uc2 "If FOMC (Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee) cuts fed funds rate tonight by 100 basis points and BOT does not cut is interest rate, it should spur capital inflows by widening the interest differential," said a Bangkok-based trader.   \par \par \uc2 Some traders cited suspected dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOT) to slow the rise in the baht, the top performer in emerging Asia that has gained 8 percent this year.   \par \par \uc2 The won, the worst performer, has lost 7.7 percent this year.   \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> fell to 7.0871 per dollar, down slightly from its post-revaluation high of 7.0815 hit on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 But analysts believe the yuan is on track to gain about 10 percent in the whole of 2008 as Beijing tries to tame inflation, which hit 8.7 percent, the highest pace in nearly 12 years.   \par \par \uc2 Premier Wen Jiabao said on Tuesday that he was confident rising prices could be controlled this year, but he conceded that the government's target of limiting annual inflation at 4.8 percent in 2008 would not be easily achieved. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0755 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        97.54         97.19       -0.36\par  Sing dlr        1.3781         1.3849       +0.49\par  Taiwan dlr      30.726         30.850       +0.40\par  Korean won     1015.10        1029.00       +1.37\par  Baht             31.17          31.38       +0.67\par  Peso             41.49          41.72       +0.55\par  Rupiah         9220.00        9300.00       +0.87\par  Rupee            40.63          40.72       +0.22\par  Ringgit         3.1695         3.1840       +0.46\par  Yuan            7.0830         7.0830       +0.00\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        97.54         111.33      +14.14\par  Sing dlr        1.3781         1.4382       +4.36\par  Taiwan dlr      30.726         32.443       +5.59\par  Korean won     1015.10         935.70       -7.82\par  Baht             31.17          33.68       +8.05\par  Peso             41.49          41.28       -0.51\par  Rupiah         9220.00        9390.00       +1.84\par  Rupee            40.63          39.41       -3.01\par  Ringgit         3.1695         3.3050       +4.28\par  Yuan            7.0830         7.3041       +3.1