\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: Clock Ticks On Baht Controls But Big Bang Unlikely   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,057 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2008\par 06:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Phisanu Phromchanya   \par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--Removal of Thailand's controversial capital controls now appears only a matter of time but the approach is likely to be step by step, rather than a big bang, to minimize appreciation pressure on the baht.   \par \par \uc2 Investors are howling for the controls to be abolished and Thailand's new pro-investment government would be happy to oblige right away. But it also wants to avoid a messy confrontation with a reluctant \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and nervous exporters.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively over the past year to slow the baht's rise - driven by the country's gaping trade surplus - and has consistently argued its job would be even harder without the controls.   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts disagree. The requirement for 30% of short-term inflows to be held on deposit at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a year has been steadily watered down since its shock imposition in December 2006 and now has minimal effect on money flows, they say.   \par \par \uc2 "If the controls are removed I don't see foreign investors rushing in and saying: 'Thank goodness I can speculate on the baht again, we really miss that.' There are lots of other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with far less erratic policy making and to be frank, better fundamentals too," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Bank.   \par \par \uc2 Still, policymakers are likely to remain cautious. Following the return of democratic government, foreign money is again flowing into Thai stocks. Removal of the controls, though they hardly affect stocks directly, is expected to rehabilitate Thailand's reputation with investors and accelerate speculative inflows.   \par \par \uc2 "We view a full removal of capital controls as unlikely in an environment where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already having to lean heavily against THB strength,"  \uc2 JPMorgan\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Claudio Piron said in a research note.   \par \par \uc2 "A compromise to further loosen capital controls appears likely with a promise for eventual liberalization, but appetite to restore a one-tier market on onshore and offshore USD/THB is likely to remain low."   \par \par \uc2 Hoping to stem the baht's rapid gains, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  imposed the 30% reserve requirement three months after the bloodless military coup that ousted prime minister Thaksin Shinawatra in September 2006. The move triggered the largest single-day fall on Thailand's stock market and was an unmitigated disaster for the country's investment reputation.   \par \par \uc2 Expectations Dashed   \par \par \uc2 The reserve requirement has been relaxed several times and only remains in place for investment in bonds, property funds and swap transactions with local banks. But use of special accounts and hedges means investments in bonds and funds can also qualify for exemption.   \par \par \uc2 With some market participants anticipating imminent removal of the controls, the offshore dollar/baht has rallied since mid-January, narrowing the spread between offshore and onshore spot rates to less than 3% now from over 10% at the beginning of this year.   \par \par \uc2 Early Thursday, the dollar was trading onshore at a new 10-year low of THB32.85 and offshore was quoted at THB32.11-THB32.19.   \par \par \uc2 Many experts estimate that dollar onshore would immediately drop to between THB32.00 and THB32.50 if the controls were removed without offsetting measures and some say it could even fall to THB31.00.   \par \par \uc2 "The removal of capital control rules would draw some foreign inflows into the bond market for speculating on the baht and enjoying the gap between local and overseas interest rates," said Vorapak Tanyawong,  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's senior country officer for Thailand.   \par \par \uc2 If removal was done without steps such as first reducing the interest rate spread between Thailand and the U.S., the dollar would fall as much as THB1, he said.  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  currently forecasts the dollar onshore will be at THB32.50 by the end of 2008.   \par \par \uc2 Local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said most market participants have already factored in the possibility that the controls will be removed and exporters have unloaded much of their dollar holdings, fearing a spike in the baht when the capital controls are dropped.   \par \par \uc2 But expectations of immediate removal were dashed Tuesday following a meeting between new Finance Minister Surapong Suebwonglee and Bank of Thailand Governor Tarisa Watanagase.   \par \par \uc2 Surapong said he needs more information from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  but promised to announce a decision before the government holds a policy roadshow that's slated for March or April.   \par \par \uc2 "The signs are really there that one way or the other, the clock is ticking on the reserve requirement," said  \uc2 Westpac\uc2 's Callow.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think it will take too much longer even if the Bank of Thailand resists. It was never a permanent solution. The new government has a mandate and an intention to present a friendlier face to the world," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Phased Removal   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts think removal of the controls will be a phased event that may not begin until the second half of the year and will be preceded by interest rate cuts that minimize the interest rate gap between Thailand and the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered economist Usara Wilaipich expects the reserve requirement to first be lifted for investment in bonds as the government's funding requirement is set to increase.   \par \par \uc2 She said there's about a 50% probability that this will happen in the second half, giving the Bank of Thailand plenty of time to match aggressive easing by the U.S. Federal Reserve.   \par \par \uc2 Usara's second scenario, for which she sees a 40% chance, involves the reserve requirement being removed in the near future and the Bank of Thailand making an inter-meeting cut to the policy rate of at least a quarter percentage point. The bank's next policy rate meeting is slated for Feb. 27.   \par \par \uc2 By the second half of the year the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also be under less pressure to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively as an expected pickup in domestic demand will help reduce the trade surplus.   \par \par \uc2 "We expect the Bank of Thailand to remain vigilant in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly will be less and less once imports start to pick up," said  \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Frederic Neumann.   \par \par \uc2 -By Phisanu Phromchanya, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 (Supunnabul Suwannakij and Stephen Wright contributed to this story.) [ 14-02-08 0511GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080214e42e0009n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
