\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asians fall, but Thai baht at 10-yr high   \b0\par\par\uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2008\par 04:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 21 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell on Monday, led by the South Korean won and the Malaysian ringgit, as world share markets continued to slide because of fears of a possible U.S. recession.   \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> bucked the trend and hit a 10-year high after news on the weekend that the party backed by ousted Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra had won enough support from coalition partners to form the next government. Thai stocks also gained. Elsewhere, investors shunned risky assets after U.S. President George W. Bush's plan of temporary tax cuts and other measures worth up to $150 billion to aid the faltering economy failed to assuage fears of a downturn.   \par \par \uc2 A recent spate of dismal earnigns reports by major banks hit by write-downs linked to the subprime crisis, also added to market gloom.   \par \par \uc2 "There is uncertainty as banks worldwide are reporting losses, including China," said Thio Chin Loo, strategist at  \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 Stocks in Asia excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> fell more than 4 percent, putting pressure on the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Foreign investors in the equity market are selling shares, which has continued from the start of this year. This acts as a buying pressure for the dollar," said Nam Kyu Kim, a trader at ING Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 South Korea's won <KRW=> extended losses against the dollar and fell more than 0.6 percent to hit 948.7 per dollar after local stocks fell <.KS11> 3 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> fell 0.7 percent to 3.2833 per dollar while local stocks lost 1.8 percent.   \par \par \uc2 BAHT, YUAN BUCK TREND   \par \par \uc2 Buoyed by the political news, the Thai baht <THB=TH> in onshore trades matched a 10-year high hit last week at 33.00 per dollar, but a suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped any further gains.   \par \par \uc2 "I suspect that BOT (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  today, at 33.03 first then all the way up," said a Bangkok-based trader.   \par \par \uc2 Assistant BOT Governor Suchada Kirakul said on Monday that the baht was rising too fast due to exporters' dollar sales and capital controls were still needed.   \par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> was underpinned by exporters' eagerness to convert their dollar revenues to yuan, but it traded weaker than Friday's close of 7.2418. The Indian rupee <INR=IN> slipped due to a sharp drop in stocks and fell 0.5 percent after the stock exchange's benchmark <.BSESN> tumbled 6.9 percent.    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0858 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.17         107.06       +0.84\par  Sing dlr        1.4437         1.4378       -0.41\par  Taiwan dlr      32.346         32.315       -0.10\par  Korean won      948.40         942.60       -0.61\par  Baht             33.10          33.03       -0.21\par  Peso             41.15          40.87       -0.68\par  Rupiah         9440.00        9433.00       -0.07\par  Rupee            39.51          39.29       -0.54\par  Ringgit         3.2850         3.2600       -0.76\par  Yuan            7.2363         7.2418       +0.08\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106