\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia retreats as stocks fall, eyes jobs data   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2007\par 03:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 2 (Reuters) - Sliding stocks gave investors an excuse to book profits from recent rallies in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, while investors turned to U.S. jobs data later in the day for fresh clues on the Federal Reserve's monetary policy.   \par \par \uc2 The high-yielding Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> fell as far as 9,140 per dollar, down almost 0.6 percent from late Asian trade on Thursday to a one-week low.   \par \par \uc2 "I think the rupiah is just following other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have fallen across the board," said a trader in Jakarta.   \par \par \uc2 "But there is a strong resistance for the dollar around 9,150, it needs to break that level if we want to see it higher. Otherwise it will stay around the current level," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> fell as far as 3.35 per dollar, down nearly 0.6 percent from late Asian trade on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> slipped to 1.4538 to the U.S. dollar, down almost 0.4 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> tumbled 2.6 percent on Thursday after brokerages downgraded the two biggest U.S. banks -- Citibank and  \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  , sparking fears of more credit crisis fallout.   \par \par \uc2 MSCI's measure of Asia Pacific stocks excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> lost 2.7 percent, a day after it hit a record high after an expected Federal Reserve interest rate cut.   \par \par \uc2 Trading was subdued as investors awaited U.S. jobs data due at 1230 GMT that could influence views on what the Federal Reserve could do at its next policy meeting in December.   \par \par \uc2 A consensus forecast of economists polled by Reuters ahead of the Labor Department's October employment report is for a modest gain of 80,000 jobs.   \par \par \uc2 Many analysts remain bullish about the underlying strength of most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although they caution about potential risks from negative news on the world's largest economy.   \par \par \uc2 "Our risk appetite indicator has been in risk loving territory since the time of the first Fed cut back in September," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank.   \par \par \uc2 "The question is how much sentiment is dented by the bad news from the U.S. overnight and of course how the payrolls turn out."   \par \par \uc2 REACTIVE TO STOCKS   \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Fed cut its key interest rate by a quarter-point to 4.5 percent to shield the economy from risks from the credit market problems, which followed a hefty half-point rate cut on Sept. 18.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed sought to moderate expectations for further rate cuts by saying inflation risks were equal to risks to economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 Philip Wee, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at DBS Bank, said some high-flying Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were prone to correction after making steep gains since the Fed's rate cut in September.   \par \par \uc2 "I reckon the market is still committed to a weaker dollar in Asia. But in the short term, it's reactive and very sensitive to any weakness in equities," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=>, another high yielder, has gained nearly 5.5 percent since Sept. 18, while the ringgit has gained about 4 percent and the Singapore dollar has risen 3.8 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Philippine markets remained closed for public holidays.   \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> briefly slipped blow the 34-per-dollar level as investor sentiment took a beating from falling stocks while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspectedly buying dollars.   \par \par \uc2 "As stock markets plunge around the world, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes the opportunity to actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the baht below 34 again," said a trader in Bangkok. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on the day at  0731 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.59         115.66       +0.93\par  Sing dlr        1.4509         1.4483       -0.18\par  Taiwan dlr      32.433         32.390       -0.13\par  Korean won      906.80         903.40       -0.37\par  Baht             33.99          33.91       -0.24\par  Rupiah         9135.00        9087.00       -0.53\par  Rupee            39.37          39.32       -0.11\par  Ringgit         3.3420         3.3300       -0.36\par  Yuan            7.4564         7.4543       -0.03\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.59         119.01       +3.86\par  Sing dlr        1.4509         1.5337       +5.71\par  Taiwan dlr      32.433         32.585       +0.47\par  Korean won      906.80         929.50       +2.50\par  Baht             33.99          36.08       +6.15\par  Peso             43.25          49.03      +13.36\par  Rupiah         9135.00        8988.00       -1.61\par  Rupee            39.37          44.26      +12.42\par  Ringgit         3.3420         3.5270       +5.54\par  Yuan            7.4564         7.8051       +4.68\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SN