\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia up on Fed rate cut, further gains expected   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 854 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2007\par 04:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 1 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Thursday after the Federal Reserve's expected rate cut further eroded the dollar's yield appeal, while investors awaited new data on the U.S. economy and further policy easing.   \par \par \uc2 The high-yielding Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> rose as far as 9,065 per dollar, up almost 0.4 percent from late Asian trade on Wednesday to a three-week high.   \par \par \uc2 "The dollar was sold down in non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) overnight after the Fed rate cut," said a trader in Jakarta.   \par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> rose to 3.3292 per dollar, up almost a quarter of a percent from late Asian trade on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed cut its key interest rate by a quarter-point to 4.5 percent on Wednesday, which followed a hefty half-point cut on Sept. 18, but sought to temper expectations for further cuts by saying that inflation risks were equal to risks to growth.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed's comments prompted analysts to push back expectations for when another U.S. rate cut could come.   \par \par \uc2 In a Reuters poll after the decision, most economists at primary bond dealers said the Fed would keep rates on hold at its next meetings in December and January.   \par \par \uc2 The fed funds rate of 4.5 percent compares with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates of 8.25 percent in Indonesia, 3.25 percent in Thailand and 3.5 percent in Malaysia.   \par \par \uc2 Most analysts remain bullish about the near-term prospects for most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though they have already rallied since the Fed's rate cut in September.   \par \par \uc2 "The odds are that the Fed will have to ease again by another 25 basis points at their next meeting in December and most certainly over the next 9 months," said Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 BNP Paribas.\par \par \uc2 "That will be bullish for Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  whilst the dollar falls off a cliff," she said.   \par \par \uc2 The optimism was echoed by  \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  analyst Craig Chan: "We still remain data-focused -- if the (favourable) global backdrop begins to fade off, it could spark a bit of profit-taking in the market, but overall I'm pretty constructive."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Investors built heavy long positions in the Singapore dollar <SGD=>, the ringgit and South Korean won <KRW=> in the past two weeks as the U.S. dollar's broad weakness made investors pick low-risk Asian assets, according to a Reuters poll [ID:nSP81108].   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar was little changed near 1.448 to the U.S. dollar, while the won fell to the weaker side of 900 per dollar, amid officials warning against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation.   \par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were suspected of buying more than $2 billion earlier this week in an effort to limit gains in the won, according to dealers.   \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> hit 33.90 per dollar, a day after it rose past the 34-per-dollar level for the first time since early August, but was checked by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "I think the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to prevent the dollar from falling below the 33.90 support level," said a trader in Bangkok. "Otherwise, the dollar will fall to 33.80 immediately."   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong government said on Thursday that it had no plan to re-peg the Hong Kong dollar or widen its trading band against the U.S. dollar, despite rising capital inflows putting pressure on it to defend the peg through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar <HKD=> fell to a two-week low at 7.7558 per U.S. dollar, within the 7.75-7.85 band, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on Wednesday to curb its rises.   \par \par \uc2 Philippine markets are closed on Thursday and Friday for public holidays. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0826 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.66         114.94       -0.62\par  Sing dlr        1.4483         1.4482       -0.01\par  Taiwan dlr      32.380         32.410       +0.09\par  Korean won      903.40         900.40       -0.33\par  Baht             33.91          33.97       +0.18\par  Rupiah         9087.00        9100.00       +0.14\par  Rupee            39.32          39.32       +0.01\par  Ringgit         3.3300         3.3370       +0.21\par  Yuan            7.4524         7.4630       +0.14\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.66         119.01       +2.90\par  Sing dlr        1.4483         1.5337       +5.90\par  Taiwan dlr      32.380         32.585       +0.63\par  Korean won      903.40         929.50       +2.89\par  Baht             33.91          36.08       +6.40\par  Peso             43.30          49.03      +13.23\par  Rupiah         9087.00        8988.00       -1.09\par  Rupee            39.32          44.26      +12.57\par  Ringgit         3.3300         3.5270       +5.92\par  Yuan            7.4524         7.8051 