\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST - Thai newspapers - July 5   \b0\par\par\uc2 363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2007\par 03:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, July 5 (Reuters) - These are some of the leading stories in Thai newspapers on Thursday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy.   \par \par \uc2 TOP STORIES   \par \par \uc2 - Help has been sought from Interpol to find the whereabouts of ousted Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra, national police chief General Seripisut Temiyavej said (THE NATION)   \par \par \uc2 - Suvarnabhumi airport is unsafe with checks on people and their belongings in the passenger terminal falling below standard, the International Air Transport Association says (BANGKOK POST)   \par \par \uc2 - The proposed National Security Act will usher Thailand back into a totalitarian era and must be resisted by all means, academics said (THE NATION)   \par \par \uc2 - Several National Legislative Assembly members slammed the government for its request for a central fund of 245.77 billion baht (BANGKOK POST)   \par \par \uc2 - Television producers have come out in protest against proposed new broadcasting restrictions, which they say will rob them of the freedom to broadcast programmes suited to target audiences (BANGKOK POST)   \par \par \uc2 BUSINESS   \par \par \uc2 - A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  expert believes the baht could rise to 30 to the U.S. dollar due to market mechanisms if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOT) does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while exporters urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the baht's appreciation (THE NATION)   \par \par \uc2 - Thai stocks rose another 1.47 percent as foreign investors continued to increase their holdings of blue-chip stocks (BANGKOK POST)   \par \par \uc2 - Fred Kin