\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thailand sees no need to act on baht rise   \b0\par\par\uc2 190 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2007\par 07:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, detail)   \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, July 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees no need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the baht despite its rise to a 10-year high onshore on Tuesday, Deputy Bank of Thailand Governor Atchana Waiquamdee told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 The baht's sharp rise against the dollar was in line with other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said.   \par \par \uc2 "Overall, it is not different from regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is still in line with regionals. We do not need to enter and act on it provided it does not overshoot or undershoot. No need to watch it every second," Atchana said.   \par \par \uc2 The baht's accelerated rise on Tuesday, after an advance the previous day, was induced partly by Thai exporters taking profit on its gains.   \par \par \uc2 The baht traded at around 34.33/35 per dollar at 0551 GMT against 34.51/53 late on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The baht has risen onshore about 4.65 percent against the dollar so far this year after surging nearly 14 percent in 2006.   \par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/BAHT (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070703e3730008p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
