\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Fin Min: Cap Inflow Rules Are A Short-Term Measure  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,206 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2006\par 12:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Thailand's imposition of a 30% reserve withholding on many capital inflows is meant to be a short-term measure to reduce volatility in the baht, which was at nine-year highs and heading higher before the move was introduced, Finance Minister Pridiyathorn Devakula said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 However, any decision to revoke the measure would be up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Pridiyathorn told Dow Jones Newswires and the Associated Press in an interview. [ 22-12-06 1148GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 "This (withholding requirement for capital inflows) is a short-term measure. It's fully within the authority of the Bank of Thailand governor (to ease or revoke the measure). With approval of the monetary policy committee, they can change it at any time. They have the liberty and flexibility to correct (adjust it to market conditions) it at any time," Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 Asked what criteria the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would use to decide when to revoke the withholding requirement, the finance minister said it would happen when the objectives had been achieved and the baht was moving back into line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of regional trading partners.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not the level of the baht, it's the level of appreciation (in the baht). If \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of our (export) competitors appreciate more or less at the same level as ours, I don't think we would need this measure any more," Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 He said it would be a great help if China allowed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise against the dollar, so that other country's with more flexible exchange rate regimes wouldn't have to shoulder so much of the burden of the dollar's necessary adjustment to the huge U.S. trade and budget deficits.  \par \par \uc2 "It would help a lot (if China revalued the yuan), but it would depend on the degree of revaluation," Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 Speculative investors who have been targeting Thailand will likely shift towards other countries with even more flexible exchange rates, he said.  \par \par \uc2 There are many ways that those countries can try to mitigate the effects of the overwhelming flows, but they may be looking at Thailand's measures, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The baht had reached a level prior to the initial imposition of the measures Tuesday where Thailand's export competitiveness was being affected, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Early Monday, the baht hit a nine-year high of THB35.06 to the dollar, up 17% since the beginning of the year.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to act because, prior to the withholding requirement, capital inflows were so huge that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't enough, Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  came up with a new set of measures Dec. 4, under which short-term trading of debt was banned and restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and loans between onshore and offshore institutions were tightened, but they proved to be ineffective, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Net capital inflows in the first week of December tripled relative to the already high levels seen in November and earlier in the year, as investors ramped up their one-way bets on a rising baht and falling interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 So the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to impose severe measures, Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 The finance minister and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said separately earlier Friday that they were satisfied with the easing in the baht, which closed the Asian session Friday at THB36.44 to the dollar, almost flat from THB36.43 Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Implementation of the measures didn't go smoothly.  \par \par \uc2 The Stock Exchange of Thailand index lost 15% Tuesday, more than on any other single day in its history, as foreigners sold a net THB25 billion worth of shares, according to the bourse.  \par \par \uc2 Pridiyathorn and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials met with market participants to work out a way to separate equities-related inflows from other inflows and exempt them from the withholding, and the changes were announced the same evening.  \par \par \uc2 "We switched it very fast because we care about foreign investors," the finance minister said.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand has allowed onshore institutions to set up special nonresident accounts for securities, which are exempt from withholding, but the funds in the accounts can be used only for trading in equities.  \par \par \uc2 Inflows related to foreign direct investment, property, tourism, education, medical, transportation, diplomatic and myriad other areas are also exempt from the withholding, but documentation must be supplied to onshore institutions as evidence for transactions worth more than $20,000.  \par \par \uc2 However, offshore financial institutions are still reluctant to trade in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with onshore institutions due to the burden of documentation, and many equities analysts are still predicting sluggish inflows and possibly heavy outflows in the months to come due to the perceived unpredictability of Thailand's regulators.  \par \par \uc2 Pridiyathorn said the policy was crystal clear.  \par \par \uc2 "The policy is still there and clear. It is intended to reduce capital inflows that put pressure on the baht (beyond those applied to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of regional trading partners)," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The ones who don't believe in our policy just sold and didn't buy back, and they made losses. Of course they are upset, and when they are upset they will criticize anything," Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 "Once we get rid of these measures (when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is back in balance), I think the whole world will understand," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Long-term investors would also have been upset if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did nothing, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Exports are a key contributor to economic growth and the stock exchange. If exports are affected, we don't think investors will like it. If we did nothing and the baht kept rising, we might see exports drop for the first time in history," he said. Since the capital inflow measures are helping to save the baht, exports won't be hurt, and economic growth can be sustained, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry's forecast for gross domestic product growth will remain at 4.5%-5% next year, compared with an estimated 5% this year, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "If there was no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the baht kept rising, we would have to revise the GDP growth estimate downward," Pridiyathorn said.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, inflows intended for investment in Thai debt instruments are still subject to the 30% withholding.  \par \par \uc2 The minister said that Thailand's current account is currently in surplus, indicating that savings are greater than investment, so foreign inflows won't be needed in the bond market for a while. While the measures are intended to be temporary, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's high foreign reserves - at $64.7 billion as of Dec. 15 - mean that it will be able to keep the measures in place for as long as necessary, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds suffered heavy losses initially, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying calmed the market, and local corporate and interbank demand are sufficient to absorb moderate supply. The bid on the benchmark 10-year bond due July 2016 closed Friday unchanged from the previous day at 5.10%, and up from 4.87% Monday. Bank of Thailand has said that most of the hot money in debt instruments is in short-term commercial paper and bills of exchange.  \par \par \uc2 -By Phisanu Phromchanya, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com [ 22-12-06 1345GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061222e2cm000kh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
