\par\par \b \uc2 Dlr Falls To 9-Year Low Vs Thai Baht At THB35.43  \b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2006\par 02:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht rose early Tuesday to a nine-year high against the U.S. dollar, hitting THB35.43 for the first time since October 1997.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the dollar selling originated in Singapore, but the reason for the sales wasn't immediately apparent.  \par \par \uc2 There were also no signs in early trade of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  said recently that it has been buying dollars on many occasions to slow the baht's rise, and it has threatened to introduce further measures to restrict the supply of baht to offshore speculators.  \par \par \uc2 The latest moves by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned short-term trading of local bonds by nonresidents, and expanded restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and loans between onshore and offshore financial institutions.  \par \par \uc2 At the Asian close Friday, the dollar was at THB35.565. Thai markets were closed Monday for a public holiday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; tom.fox@dowjones.com [ 12-12-06 0114GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061212e2cc0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
