\par\par \b \uc2 Thai exporters feeling pain as baht soars  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Darren Schuettler  \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2006\par 11:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Dec 8 (Reuters) - From textiles to tuna, rice to rubber, Thai exporters are feeling the pain of a soaring baht, Asia's strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has also tested the mettle of the new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor.  \par \par \uc2  The baht <THB=> has risen nearly 6 percent since the Sept. 19 coup that ousted Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra and 15.6 percent since January. That compares with a rise of 10 percent in the Korean won <KRW=> and 8.4 percent in the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=>.  \par \par \uc2  Some analysts expect the baht, which was trading at 35.56 per dollar on Friday, to strengthen further in the next 3-6 months despite Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and new anti-speculation measures announced on Monday.  \par \par \uc2  That's grim news for exporters of footwear, foods, textiles and electronics who pay some or all of their costs in baht and earn the bulk of their income in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including that of the United States, Thailand's biggest trading partner.  \par \par \uc2  Thai Union Frozen Products PCL , Asia's largest canned tuna exporter with its popular "Chicken of the Sea" brand, was the latest company to issue a profit warning, saying the strong baht would hit earnings and revenue in 2007.  \par \par \uc2  "We have been trying to control costs, improve efficiency and boost sales to minimise the impact of the strong baht," TUF President Thiraphong Chansiri told Reuters on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2  Last month, a coalition of food and textile exporters demanded action to stem the baht's rise or compensation from the government to offset rising costs.  \par \par \uc2  "If the baht continues to rise, members will have to cut their production, employment and finally, shut down their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It will strongly affect farmers and labourers," 10 export associations said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2  PACKING UP?  \par \par \uc2  Commerce Minister Krirk-krai Jirapaet said on Thursday the government was considering tax breaks and other incentives to help embattled manufacturers. He gave no details.  \par \par \uc2  The ministry also cut its 2007 export growth target to 12.5 percent due to the stronger baht from a previous estimate of 15 percent and compared to 16 percent growth in 2006.  \par \par \uc2  Some exporters have urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates to stem strong capital inflows that have buoyed the baht and pushed down bond yields in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2  But Governor Tarisa Watanangase, facing her first major test since assuming the top post in October, has ruled out an immediate rate cut, saying that might draw more money into the country from investors expecting it to stimulate the economy.  \par \par \uc2  Instead, the new measures to deter speculators from buying short-term debt should be given time to work, she said.  "If the rules introduced on Monday are not enough, there will be more measures," Tarisa said, without giving details.  \par \par \uc2  Economist Supavud Saicheua of Phatra Securities, which forecast the baht would rise another two percent by the second quarter of 2007, said it remained to be seen if the new rules would work.  "If these measures fail to stem short-term inflows, the BOT will consider taxing certain short-term financial transactions," he said in a report.  \par \par \uc2  Kasikorn Research Center, which expected the baht to touch 35.0 in the first half of 2007, said exporters could diversify their export markets, reduce dollar-denominated trade or even outsource production to other countries.  \par \par \uc2  "They may have to consider relocating some manufacturing plants abroad where production costs are lower or will be less affected by the declining trend of the U.S. dollar," it said.  \par \par \uc2  But exporters are not packing their bags just yet.  \par \par \uc2  "I can't see companies shutting down \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and moving away just because of this," said Richard Han, chief executive of Hana Microelectronics PCL .  \par \par \uc2  Han, whose firm assembles integrated circuit packages for mobile phones and hand-held computers and pays 35-40 percent of its costs in baht, said "people will have to get used to a weak dollar for a while to come."  \par \par \uc2  "Whatever the government can do to stop speculation is desirable, but you have to try to ride it out and improve your efficiency," he said.     \par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/EXPORTERS (PICTURE)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061208e2c8000qt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
