\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia steady, Thai baht slips on cenbank action  \b0\par\par\uc2 763 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2006\par 05:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Dec 4 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were steady versus the dollar on Monday as optimism from solid regional economic data was offset by concerns about official steps to cap \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains, which hit the Thai baht <THB=>.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand Governor Tarisa Watanagase announced measures on Monday to tighten \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules, including banning non-residents from bond repurchase deals.  \par \par \uc2 She said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take more action if its measures failed to ease pressure on the baht, which has risen about 14.5 percent against the dollar this year.[ID:nBKK283072]  \par \par \uc2 The baht fell 0.45 percent to 35.90 per dollar on Monday, backing off from Friday's eight-year high of 35.70. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves overshadowed stronger-than-expected economic growth figures. [ID:nBKK93899]  \par \par \uc2 Most analysts are optimistic about the near-term outlook for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a spate of healthy economic data, including Thailand's growth data and solid trade surpluses in South Korea and Indonesia.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the near-term outlook for the Asian market is still very good," said Irene Cheung, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "Eventhough there is a slowdown in the U.S., we believe that the regional momentum from China, India, and consumption growth are going to be supporting factors for next year," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Sustained capital inflows and trade surpluses have led to renewed rallies among Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, sparking a wave of verbal and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by regional central banks to cap further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The latest Reuters poll on positioning in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests investors stepped up long positions in the Thai baht and remained bullish on the South Korean won and Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar remained under broad selling pressure after weak U.S. factory data last week reinforced expectations the Federal Reserve would cut interest rates next year.  \par \par \uc2 BAHT DEPRESSED  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a major Thai bank said he did not spot any sign of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. dollar on Monday despite the baht's sharp fall.  \par \par \uc2 Prior to Monday's announcement, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  both verbally and through dollar buying in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell nearly a fifth of a percent to 1.5455 on Monday, off a nine-year high set last week.  \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> firmed as high as 32.25 per U.S. dollar, matching Friday's peak, which was the highest in about 4-\u189\'00\'BD months.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> firmed against the dollar for a fourth consecutive session on Monday, but the persistent speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may purchase dollars to rein in the won prevent it from rising sharply.  \par \par \uc2 The won rose as high as 926 per dollar, holding back from a nine-year high of 925 on Nov. 27.  \par \par \uc2 China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> strengthened on Monday to 7.8230 per dollar, its highest level since its July 2005 revaluation. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0822 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.63         115.95       +0.28\par  Sing dlr        1.5455         1.5429       -0.17\par  Taiwan dlr      32.388         32.370       -0.06\par  Korean won      927.40         928.50       +0.12\par  Baht             35.90          35.74       -0.45\par  Peso             49.75          49.66       -0.18\par  Rupiah         9150.00        9150.00       +0.00\par  Rupee            44.60          44.66       +0.12\par  Ringgit         3.5860         3.5930       +0.20\par  Yuan            7.8285         7.8360       +0.10\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.63         117.96       +2.02\par  Sing dlr        1.5455         1.6625       +7.57\par  Taiwan dlr      32.388         32.850       +1.43\par  Korean won      927.40        1011.60       +9.08\par  Baht             35.90          41.11      +14.51\par  Peso             49.75          53.09       +6.71\par  Rupiah         9150.00        9835.00       +7.49\par  Rupee            44.60          45.04       +0.99\par  Ringgit         3.5860         3.7790       +5.38\par  Yuan            7.8285         8.0702       +3.09 ((Reporting\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 by Kevin Yao, editing by Neil Fullick; Reuters Messaging: kevin.yao.reuters.com@reuters.net; +65 6870 3841)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the new Reuters scrolling global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  service please click <FXNEWS>  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPD