\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Philippine peso bucks Asian weakness  \b0\par\par\uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2006\par 03:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 17 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened on Friday on further unwinding of positions by traders fearing more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the dollar's strength despite the softness in U.S. inflation data.  \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht <THB=> was the biggest loser, shedding nearly 0.4 percent as it hit a one-week trough of 36.60 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=> was the lone exception to the weakness, as a heavy bunch of export earnings forced it to rally 0.2 percent to around 49.96 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2  "When spot failed to go above 50.20 yesterday, a lot of exporters placed orders to sell," said one Manila-based trader, referring to the peso's rally.  \par \par \uc2  Sean Callow, Westpac Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said the peso has traditionally been driven more by flows from trade transactions.  \par \par \uc2  "So it is less likely to participate in any knee-jerk moves that, for example, the Sing dollar and Korean won have when broad dollar sentiment changes," he said.  \par \par \uc2  But this week's decline in the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised worries about whether this is merely a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the start of a bigger sell-off of emerging markets.  \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar <SGD=> lost a quarter of a percent to hit a low of 1.5633 per U.S. dollar, taking losses from 9-year highs struck this week to half a percent and prompting speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as several other central banks did this week.  \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in the baht after it hit its strongest level in nearly seven years on Tuesday. The Reserve Bank of India and the Bank of Korea were also spotted buying dollars to halt the appreciation in their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupee <INR=IN> and the won <KRW=>.  \par \par \uc2  The won was slightly weaker at 938 per dollar on the day, but strong dollar earnings for Korean shipmakers and electronics exporters had helped lift it from lows near 944 on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> fell a quarter of a percent on Friday.  Analysts at UBS said selling of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spurred not just by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears but by warnings from central banks in New Zealand and Japan on the heavy accumulation of carry trades.  \par \par \uc2  The yen and Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> have been the favoured funding vehicles in Asia for carry trades, which involve borrowing low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to invest in high-yielding assets.  \par \par \uc2  "The combination of these has contributed to position squaring particularly in high-yielding Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," UBS said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  "The high-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -- Indonesian rupiah,\par Philippine peso, Korean won, Indian rupee -- weakened\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 significantly while the lower-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the\par Singapore dollar and Taiwan dollar have largely remained\par resilient."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The yen <JPY=> was trading near its weakest level this\par week, changing hands around 118.33/35 per dollar, as markets\par discounted tame U.S. inflation data to focus on a likely soft\par landing for the U.S. economy and steady U.S. rates for a\par while.\par   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0810 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.31         118.04       -0.23\par  Sing dlr        1.5599         1.5580       -0.12\par  Taiwan dlr      32.927         32.835       -0.28\par  Korean won      938.70         937.40       -0.14\par  Baht             36.61          36.46       -0.41\par  Peso             49.96          50.08       +0.24\par  Rupiah         9164.00        9163.00       -0.01\par  Rupee            44.92          44.90       -0.04\par  Ringgit         3.6520         3.6520       +0.00\par  Yuan            7.8738         7.8688       -0.06\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.31         117.96       -0.30\par  Sing dlr        1.5599         1.6625       +6.58\par  Taiwan dlr      32.927         32.850       -0.23\par  Korean won      938.70        1011.60       +7.77\par  Baht             36.61          41.11      +12.29\par  Peso             49.96          53.09       +6.27\par  Rupiah         9164.00        9835.00       +7.32\par  Rupee            44.92          45.04       +0.27\par  Ringgit         3.6520         3.7790       +3.48\par  Yuan            7.8738         8.0702       +2.49\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061120e2bh000e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex10